WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.400
When it comes to something
as life-and-death important

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.120
as what to feed ourselves
and our families,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.760
we should rely not on
anecdotes, but on facts.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.040
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.480
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.120
There’s a lot of research
being done right now

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.520
on the best way to understand
the processes by which we age.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.120
Here’s our first story.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.640
There are numerous ways
to try to unlock

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.720
the mysteries of aging.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.920
You could study
long-lived individuals

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.400
like centenarians
and supercentenarians,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.160
particularly long-lived smokers,
perhaps,

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.760
to uncover the secrets
to their resilience.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.600
Or, you could go
in the opposite direction

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.320
and study short-lived people—

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.240
tragic, accelerated aging
syndromes like progeria,

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.360
where children age at eight
to ten times the normal rate,

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.680
wrinkling, balding, and then
typically dying around age 13

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:55.280
of a heart attack or stroke.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.520
Or, you could study
long-lived animals.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.560
There are mammals,
such as the bowhead whale,

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.920
that can live hundreds of years.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.880
There are oysters and clams
whose hearts can beat

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.640
over a billion times
over its five-century lifespan.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.840
What accounts for the
10,000-fold range of lifespan

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.480
in the animal kingdom?

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.160
Most of the aging pathways

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.600
identified
as the hallmarks of aging

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.520
were established using
so-called "model organisms,"

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.960
such as yeast, worms,
flies, and mice,

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.600
simpler species that may
nonetheless offer insights

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.640
due to the remarkable
conservation

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.160
of common aging mechanisms

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.440
through the eons
of evolutionary time.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.400
Aging used to be considered
simply too complex to study,

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.440
a constellation of internal
and external influences

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.320
too complicated to disentangle.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.640
But then, the game changer,

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.960
the discovery
that a single gene mutation

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:51.360
could dramatically prolong
the lifespan and youthful state

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.120
of a tiny worm
known as C. elegans.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.240
C. elegans has since
wormed its way

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.160
deep into the study
of longevity.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.160
It seems we shared
a common ancestor

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.960
about a half billion years ago,
and, to this day,

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.080
we still share
about half their genes.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.200
With a lifespan of only
two to three weeks,

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.520
their fast turnover allows
researchers to rapidly assess

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.320
the effects of genetic
or dietary tweaks,

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:17.400
unlike humans,
who are described as

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.360
"not an easy-to-study system

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.240
both for ethical as well as
for practical reasons."

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:24.400
Even simpler,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.920
the single-celled organism
Saccharomyces cerevisiae,

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:31.080
otherwise known as brewer's
or baker's yeast.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.440
In 1959, it was discovered that
yeast cells are not immortal,

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:38.920
dividing only
a finite number of times.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:41.840
They're even more evolutionarily
divergent, though.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.760
We haven't shared a common
ancestor with brewer's yeast

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.880
for more like a billion years

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.400
and only have about 30 percent
of their genes in common

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.200
at this point,

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.880
but their microscopic size
and even faster turnover

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.840
allows for high-throughput
systems able to screen

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:00.080
through more than a thousand
different compounds a day

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.480
for potentially
lifespan-extending properties.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.880
And, even if those
yeast longevity compounds

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.680
fail to translate
to extending human life,

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:10.120
yeast researchers argue

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:14.440
they could still be useful
for brewing extra beer.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.360
Yeast have a lifespan
that can be measured in days,

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:18.360
worms in weeks,

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.560
fruit flies—
another common model—in months,

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:24.280
compared to mice
that can live for years.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.960
But, mice and men were one
as few as 75 million years ago,

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.080
arising
in the mammalian explosion

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.680
shortly after the resolution

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:36.040
of the asteroid v. dinosaurs
matchup.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:39.320
Mice share about 85 percent
of their genes with humans.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:42.880
Granted,
it's an important 15 percent.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:46.080
Humans are not just more complex
anatomically than rodents,

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.760
but more complex
even on a cellular level.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.880
Extrapolating data
from lab animals

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.880
is infamously fraught
with difficulty.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.880
Fewer than one in ten cancer
drugs that seem to work in mice

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.320
even make it
into human clinical trials,

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:02.360
and hundreds of seemingly
promising Alzheimer's drugs

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.560
have similarly been
lost in translation.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:09.320
As one review in the journal
Trends in Biotechnology put it:

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:13.160
"Humans Are Not Huge Worms
or Big Mice."

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:16.296
We are, however, big primates.

00:04:16.296 --> 00:04:19.160
Rhesus monkeys are also used
in aging research,

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.320
though can live
up to 40 years,

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:23.760
stretching research timelines.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.520
Their DNA is 93 percent
identical to humans,

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.000
though as similarity increases,

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.840
so too do ethical concerns
regarding experimentation.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:34.480
One might expect
research on dogs

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.360
to perhaps be most sensitive,

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.400
but there are
citizen science initiatives

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.120
in which family dogs are
enrolled in noninvasive studies

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:42.520
to study, for example,

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:46.000
the genetics of why some
so-called "methuselah dogs"

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.320
reach ages of 25 or more,

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.840
but 99.9 percent
of other dogs do not.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:55.760
For example, mixed-breed dogs
live more than a year longer

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.040
than same-size purebred dogs,

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:01.000
who can be plagued
with certain genetic disorders.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.200
Aged pooches suffer many
of the same ravages of aging:

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:07.200
arthritis, cancer, cataracts,
kidney problems, muscle loss,

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:08.440
et cetera.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.680
Advances in canine longevity

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.280
might not only be applicable
to human aging

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:14.720
but have the intrinsic value

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:17.000
of enhancing the quality
and quantity of life

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:19.480
of the more than
70 million companions

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:23.800
we share our homes with
in the U.S. alone.

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:24.520
In our next story,

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:28.960
we look at ways to boost the
longevity hormone FGF21.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:32.160
In the year 2000, a new human
hormone was discovered.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:35.880
It was the 21st documented
fibroblast growth factor;

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:39.280
so, they called it FGF21.

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:43.320
Since its discovery, FGF21 has
emerged as a key agent for

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:47.680
promotion of metabolic and artery
health, leanness, and longevity.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.640
Inject it into fat monkeys,
and they lose body weight

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.240
without reducing food intake,
and not just a little—

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.720
a 27 percent drop in body fat
eating the same amount.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:02.080
In mice, it increases their
lifespan 30 to 40 percent,

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:05.600
comparable to lifelong caloric
restriction, but again

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:09.600
was achieved without
decreasing food intake.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.440
The researchers conclude that
FGF21 could potentially be

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:16.440
used as a hormone therapy to
extend lifespan in mammals,

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:20.600
which has gotten big pharma
salivating, raising the question

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:24.760
“Can aging be ‘drugged’?”
And that’s not all it can do.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:28.560
The idea that one drug
can treat obesity, diabetes,

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:31.560
dyslipidemia (like high
cholesterol), and hypertension

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:34.880
all at once might have
seemed impossible a few years ago

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:38.440
but is now a tantalizing
and exciting prospect.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.800
The reason you can’t just give
people straight FGF21 is that

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:44.240
it gets rapidly broken down in the body;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:45.960
so, you’d have to get injections like

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:47.760
every hour or two around the clock.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:51.200
So, drug companies are
trying to patent a variety of

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:53.840
longer-acting FGF21 look-alikes.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:59.520
And indeed, give people
a little PF-05231023

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.480
and they can lose about
10 pounds in 25 days,

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:06.360
along with dramatic drops in
triglycerides and cholesterol.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:08.880
But then, the side effects
of these new-fangled drugs

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:12.600
started cropping up.
Okay, so what about this:

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:17.400
we package the FGF21 gene into
a virus and then inject the virus

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:23.080
and have it stitch extra
FGF21 genes into our DNA.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:27.920
Or you can just lace
on your running shoes.

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:31.720
Exercise boosts FGF21 levels,
which may in fact

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:35.360
be one of the reasons
exercise is so good for us.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:39.360
Which works better, though,
aerobic exercise—eight weeks

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:43.600
of running training—or resistance
exercise—eight weeks of weights?

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:47.760
The answer is both, but the
resistance exercise edged out

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:50.840
the running, a 42 percent
increase in FGF21 versus

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:54.800
a 25 percent increase in
the aerobic exercise group.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:57.800
Okay, but what can we do with food?

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:59.960
Yeah, you could try
engineering and injecting it,

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:03.520
but wouldn’t it be easier to
just stimulate our own endogenous,

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:06.600
natural production through diet?

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.440
One way is through no diet at all.

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.640
You may have noticed it’s been
dubbed the starvation hormone.

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:17.720
That’s because fasting induces
FGF21, but not just a day or two.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:21.440
Physiologically, FGF21
expression is markedly increased

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:23.240
in response to fasting/starvation.

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:26.000
But, unlike mice, which
show an increase after

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:29.840
just six hours of fasting,
humans don’t get a notable surge

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:33.160
in FGF21 until after a week.

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:38.120
Fasting can quadruple FGF21,
but it takes 10 days of fasting,

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:45.409
which is the very poster child of
an unsustainable eating pattern.

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:46.560
Finally today,

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:51.280
what can physicians do to promote healthy,
life-extending, lifestyle changes?

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:53.560
Here’s the story.

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:57.000
A pivotal paper published in
Europe more than a decade ago

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.400
entitled “Healthy Living Is
the Best Revenge” found that

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.760
adhering to just four simple
healthy lifestyle factors

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:06.560
compared to none could
potentially have a strong impact

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:09.080
on the prevention
of chronic diseases;

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:12.240
we’re talking nearly 80 percent
less chronic disease risk,

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:14.680
slashing diabetes
risk by 93 percent,

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:17.160
dropping heart attack
risk by 81 percent,

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:21.360
and cutting stroke risk in half,
and cancer by 36 percent.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.120
Think about what that means.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:26.680
The potential for preventing
disease and death is enormous.

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:28.840
In the U.S. alone every year,
there are half million

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:32.240
heart attacks, half million
strokes, a million new cases

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:36.120
of diabetes, and a
million new cancer diagnoses.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:39.760
The message is clear:
adopting a few healthy behaviors

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:42.680
can have a major impact.

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:44.680
What are those four fabled factors?

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:48.800
Never smoking, not being obese,
averaging about a half hour of

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:53.080
exercise a day, and adhering to
healthy dietary principles,

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:56.640
like lots of fruits, vegetables,
and whole grains, and less meat.

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.520
Follow those four
simple rules and boom!

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:04.280
Enjoy nearly 80 percent reduced
risk of major chronic diseases.

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:07.200
What does that mean
for mortality risk?

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:08.880
A similar batch of four healthy

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:10.240
behaviors combined predicted

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:13.200
a four-fold difference
in total mortality,

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.480
with an estimated impact
equivalent to 14 years

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:19.840
in chronological age, meaning
the individuals were dying at

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:25.040
such a reduced rate that it was
as if they were 14 years younger.

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:29.600
“Finally, a Regimen to
Extend Human Life Expectancy.”

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:32.120
This commentary was in
reference to this study,

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:34.600
where a similar analysis
of the impact of healthy

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:37.080
lifestyle behaviors on
life expectancies was made,

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:40.600
but this time it looked
directly at the U.S. population,

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:43.320
which is particularly important,
since Americans have a

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:45.720
shorter life expectancy
compared with residents

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:48.720
of nearly all other
high-income countries.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.440
The researchers concluded that
adopting a healthy lifestyle

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:54.280
could substantially
reduce premature mortality

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:58.400
and prolong life
expectancy in U.S. adults.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:00.400
Okay, but by how much?

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:03.520
They estimated that adherence
to a low-risk lifestyle

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:07.840
could prolong life expectancy
at age 50 by 14 years

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:11.080
in women and 12.2 years in men.

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:14.560
So, if you’re 50 right now,
instead of only living to 79

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:18.040
if you’re a woman, and
75½ if you’re a man,

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:21.760
taking even just basic care
of yourself could propel you

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:24.320
to an average life expectancy
of 93 if you’re a woman

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:26.880
and 87½ if you’re a man.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:31.800
The bottom line is it’s never
too late to turn back the clock.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:35.120
A midlife switch just
to the basics, like at least

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:37.280
five servings fruits
and vegetables a day,

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:39.600
walking even like 20 minutes a day,

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:42.000
maintaining a healthy
weight, and not smoking

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.760
results in a substantial
reduction in mortality

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:47.280
even in the following few years.

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:49.720
We’re talking a 40 percent
lower risk of dying

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:51.360
in the subsequent four years.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:54.400
So, making the necessary
changes to adhere to a healthy

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:57.840
lifestyle could be considered
extremely worthwhile,

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:01.680
and middle age is certainly
not too late to act.

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:05.440
As an aside, realizing the
12 to 14 years of added life

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:08.440
study was based on data
from health professionals,

00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:11.440
that got me excited about
all the potential knock-on effects.

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:14.200
If health professionals
start getting healthier,

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:16.800
they could become role models
for more healthful living

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:19.800
and potentially save more
lives than just their own.

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:22.680
But that may have just
been wishful thinking.

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:26.680
Practicing what you preach
can sometimes backfire.

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:32.280
Evidently, “displays of excellence
can paradoxically turn off

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:35.720
the very people they
are trying to inspire.”

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:38.200
You’d assume that not
being a hypocrite and trying

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:41.240
to walk the walk would just
lead to positive consequences,

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:43.480
inspiring confidence in others, right?

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:47.400
Don’t you want a dance instructor
who can dance, a music teacher

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:51.440
who can play, a health
professional who’s healthy?

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:54.520
But this simple intuition
fails to take into account

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:58.920
concerns about making
other people feel inadequate.

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:01.440
Like you know how vegetarians
are the targets of a

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:03.960
surprising amount of
hostility and ridicule.

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:06.800
That’s because they may
come off as morally superior

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:09.680
and make people feel like
they’re being looked down upon.

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:12.000
There was an elegant
demonstration of this phenomenon

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:15.160
in this study where
“Principled deviants who take

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:18.560
the high road threaten
others’ moral self-worth.”

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:21.320
This is what they did.
Research subjects were asked

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:24.760
to complete a racist
task by an experimenter,

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:28.960
and those moral rebels who
refused to obey were cheered

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:33.840
by observers but were
disparaged by participants

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:36.680
who had themselves
gone through with it,

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:40.720
and for whom the rebel’s stance
thus implied an indictment

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:43.320
of their spinelessness.
Isn’t that interesting?

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:47.160
So, when doctors portray themselves
as “the picture of health,”

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:50.040
patients might think they’re
being holier-than-thou and

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:53.160
unintentionally alienate people,
potentially turning off

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:56.240
the very patients who
most need their help.

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:59.080
You can see how someone with
a weight issue might feel

00:13:59.080 --> 00:14:02.560
threatened and judged
by a physician triathlete.

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:04.280
But what are we supposed to do?

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:06.200
We want healthy practitioners.

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:08.640
Physicians who
smoke are less likely

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:10.560
to tell their patients
to quit smoking,

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:12.120
physicians who are overweight

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:14.520
are less likely to counsel
their patients about weight,

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:16.000
and physicians
who don’t work out

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:19.080
are less likely to
talk about exercise.

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:22.520
What we can do to make
patients more comfortable

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:25.640
is emphasize that our
role is to help people

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:30.360
meet their own personal health
goals, whatever they may be.

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:34.120
Studies show that when doctors
do this, take this approach,

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:37.600
it increases the appeal of
fitness-focused physicians

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:39.240
to overweight patients.

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:41.600
So, we can then display
exemplary behavior

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:45.080
while at the same time
not inadvertently alienate

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:50.200
the very people who could
benefit most from our guidance.

